<BaseLayout title="Fall Colors from Vancouver to Whistler — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Four days in British Columbia at the end of September 2023: the Vancouver waterfront, the Capilano Suspension Bridge, the Sea-to-Sky Highway at sunset, red maples and the Olympic rings in Whistler, and Green Lake.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · September–October 2023</p>
  <h1>Fall Colors from Vancouver to Whistler</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Four days in British Columbia: the harbour, a bridge over a canyon, the Sea-to-Sky Highway and Whistler in red.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 days · September 29 – October 2, 2023</strong><br />
    Lunch in Richmond → UBC → Gastown → Canada Place and Coal Harbour → the Vancouver Lookout → hotpot on Robson Street →
    the Capilano Suspension Bridge → the Sea-to-Sky Highway at sunset → Whistler Village → Green Lake → Stanley Park →
    Richmond → home<br />
    One country, one province · one suspension bridge · one set of Olympic rings · one green lake · one panda in a drink
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the Vancouver area and the Sea-to-Sky corridor marking Richmond, UBC, downtown Vancouver, Capilano, Howe Sound, the Stawamus Chief, Whistler Village, Green Lake and Stanley Park, joined by dashed arrows in the order visited." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole trip: Vancouver, then up the Sea-to-Sky Highway to Whistler and back.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    At the end of September 2023 my wife and I spent four days in British Columbia: most of it in Vancouver, one night in
    Whistler, and a last day in Richmond before the flight home. The two of us were together the whole way. Our
    two phones have photos from the same places within minutes of each other on every day of the trip, and most of
    the photos below are hers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first photos on my wife's phone are from LAX on the evening of Thursday, September 28th. The first ones on
    mine are from lunch in Richmond, near Vancouver's airport, the next day. Everything below is put together from
    the two camera rolls: what was photographed, where, and at what minute.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Richmond, UBC and the harbour</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0929.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0929.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1, from lunch in Richmond north to UBC, then east to BC Place, the Gastown steam clock, Canada Place and the Digital Orca on the downtown waterfront." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Friday: from Richmond to UBC and the downtown waterfront.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The trip started with dim sum. A little after one o'clock on Friday we were at a table in Richmond with a steamer
    of pale green buns, egg tarts and a bowl of rice, among other plates. Before we left Richmond we picked up two
    cups of tea from Lovin Tea.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'richmond-dim-sum.webp'} alt="A dim sum table: a bamboo steamer of three pale green buns, two egg tarts, a plate of food in a brown sauce and a bowl of rice with toppings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dim sum in Richmond, 1:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we drove up to the University of British Columbia, at the western tip of the city. The fountain in
    Martha Piper Plaza, at 3:08 pm, is in my <a href="/misc/campuses">campuses post</a>, so I won't repeat it here. A
    little later, at 3:45, my wife photographed a tall totem pole on a lawn, with a carved
    bird at the top spreading its wings against a clear blue sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'ubc-totem.webp'} alt="A tall carved totem pole topped by a bird with spread wings, against a clear blue sky, with trees below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A totem pole at UBC, 3:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By five we were downtown. Outside BC Place, the stadium with the white cable-supported roof, a long queue of
    people stretched along the street. From there we walked north to <strong>Gastown</strong>, where a small crowd
    stood around the steam clock on the corner. Behind it, down the street, the North Shore
    mountains were in the sun.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'gastown-steam-clock.webp'} alt="The Gastown steam clock, a dark metal clock tower with a lit clock face, beside a red brick building, with mountains in the distance under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Gastown steam clock, 5:52 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A few minutes later, from near the waterfront, the view opened up across the harbour to North Vancouver: a band
    of white towers along the shore, a red cargo ship in the water, and the mountains behind, with cloud catching
    on the tops.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'north-shore.webp'} alt="North Vancouver across the harbour: white apartment towers along the shore, a red cargo ship on the water, and forested mountains behind under scattered cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>North Vancouver across the harbour, 5:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Just after six we reached <strong>Canada Place</strong> and the glass Vancouver Convention Centre next to it, which
    sits right on the water. Further along the seawall, floatplanes were tied up at their docks and a green-and-white passenger
    ferry was beside them.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canada-place.webp'} alt="The glass Vancouver Convention Centre on its pier over deep blue water, with the North Shore mountains in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Convention Centre on the water, 6:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'harbour-floatplanes.webp'} alt="Floatplanes at their docks in the harbour and a green-and-white passenger ferry, with Stanley Park and the North Shore mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Floatplanes and a ferry, 6:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last stop of the evening was Jack Poole Plaza, beside the convention centre, where <strong>Digital Orca</strong>
    stands: a killer whale made of black and white cubes, as if it had been built out of pixels, jumping in front of
    the glass.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'digital-orca.webp'} alt="A sculpture of a leaping killer whale made of black and white cubes in front of a glass building, with a red umbrella in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Digital Orca, 6:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: The Lookout, hotpot, Capilano and the Sea-to-Sky</h2>
  <p>
    On Saturday we went up the <strong>Vancouver Lookout</strong>, the observation deck on top of Harbour Centre, a
    little after one. Two of my photos from up there are in <a href="/misc/views-from-above">Views from Up High</a>.
    My wife's are different. Straight down to the north were the white sails of Canada Place on their pier, with the
    harbour and the North Shore mountains beyond. To the south, the white roof of BC Place, the stadium we had passed
    the evening before, sat among the towers.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lookout-canada-place.webp'} alt="Canada Place from high above: the white sail roofs on a long pier, the blue harbour with ships, and forested mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Canada Place from the Lookout, 1:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lookout-bc-place.webp'} alt="Downtown Vancouver from above, with the white roof of BC Place among office towers and a brick tower with a green roof in the middle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>BC Place from the Lookout, 1:15 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch was hotpot on Robson Street, with a split pot of two broths in the middle of the table and a big ring of
    thinly sliced meat laid all the way around it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hotpot.webp'} alt="A split hotpot with a dark broth on one side and a red broth on the other, surrounded by a ring of rolled slices of raw beef." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotpot on Robson Street, 2:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we drove across the Lions Gate Bridge to North Vancouver and the <strong>Capilano Suspension
    Bridge</strong> park. Just inside, at 4:36, three carved poles with painted faces stood among the trees. Fifteen
    minutes later we were on the bridge itself, a long, narrow walkway of cables and wire mesh hung across the
    Capilano River canyon. It was busy. From the middle, the river ran
    far below between steep walls of tall evergreens, with a few trees on the slopes turning yellow.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capilano-totems.webp'} alt="Three carved wooden poles with painted faces standing among ferns and trees beside a path of stepping stones." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Carved poles near the entrance, 4:36 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'capilano-bridge.webp'} alt="A long narrow suspension bridge with wire-mesh sides stretching into a forest, with people walking on it in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Capilano Suspension Bridge, 4:55 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The canyon is deep and narrow, and from the walkway you look over the cable handrail straight down at the
    river.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'capilano-canyon.webp'} alt="Looking down from the suspension bridge over its cable handrail and wire mesh to a river in a deep, narrow canyon of tall evergreens." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Capilano River from the bridge, 4:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the far side the paths go on into the forest. At 5:21 a woman was standing by the path with an owl on her arm,
    and at 5:30 my wife photographed a set of much smaller suspended
    walkways strung high between the trunks of the trees. At 5:41 I photographed a framed certificate
    on display: "I survived the world-famous Capilano Suspension Bridge."
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'capilano-treetops.webp'} alt="Tall evergreen trees with a narrow suspended walkway strung between them high above the ground, against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Walkways in the treetops, 5:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we drove north on the <strong>Sea-to-Sky Highway</strong> to Whistler. My wife filmed short videos from the
    car, and the frames below come from them. At 6:21 Howe Sound lay below the road,
    flat and pale, with a small wooded island in the middle.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'howe-sound.webp'} alt="Howe Sound from the highway: calm, pale water with a small wooded island and mountains on both sides, beyond a concrete road barrier." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Howe Sound from the Sea-to-Sky Highway, 6:21 pm (from a video).</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By 6:48 we were near Squamish, and the
    <strong>Stawamus Chief</strong>, the great bare rock dome above the town, stood up beside the road. A few minutes
    later the top of a snow-covered peak ahead had turned pink while everything
    below it was already in shade.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stawamus-chief.webp'} alt="The bare rock dome of the Stawamus Chief rising above forest beside a highway, under a pale evening sky, with a power pylon on the left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Stawamus Chief, 6:48 pm (from a video).</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pink-peak.webp'} alt="A snow-covered mountain peak lit pink in the evening light above dark forested hills, with power lines and a road below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last light on a snowy peak, 6:51 pm (from a video).</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Our hotel for the night was the Hilton Whistler Resort &amp; Spa, booked two days before. There are no photos of the
    rest of the drive.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 3: Whistler in red, Green Lake, and back to Stanley Park</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday was the day of fall colors. At 1:20 pm, from near the hotel, Whistler Mountain stood across the valley,
    green with forest, with the lines of ski runs down its face and a dusting of snow along the top. A few minutes
    later we passed the stone <strong>inukshuk</strong> by the road into the village. The sign under it, in French, calls
    Whistler the host mountain resort of the Olympic and Paralympic Games.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'whistler-mountain.webp'} alt="Whistler Mountain across the valley, forested, with ski runs cut down its face and a little snow on the ridge, above chalet-style buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Whistler Mountain, 1:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'inukshuk.webp'} alt="A large inukshuk of stacked stone slabs on a lawn, with a Whistler sign in French at its base and mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The inukshuk, 1:23 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the village itself, the maples had turned. On the Forest Walk, at 1:28, a small pond sat under trees that were
    bright red from top to bottom, and the ground around it was covered in fallen red leaves.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'forest-walk-maples.webp'} alt="A small pond with stepping stones under bright red maple trees, with fallen red leaves covering the ground in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Red maples on the Forest Walk, 1:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A quarter of an hour later we were in Olympic Plaza, where the <strong>Olympic rings</strong> stand in front of a
    clock tower, with a bright red maple beside them. The Village
    Stroll was busy with people, and the trees along it were red and orange between the dark pines.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'olympic-rings.webp'} alt="The silver Olympic rings on a stone base in a plaza, with a clock tower and a large bright red maple behind, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Olympic rings, 1:44 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 2:25 we stopped at a pullout on the Sea-to-Sky Highway just north of the village, above
    <strong>Green Lake</strong>. The water really is green: a milky jade color under a row of peaks with fresh snow
    on their tops, and the highway curving along the shore below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'green-lake.webp'} alt="Green Lake, milky green water below forested slopes and a row of mountains with fresh snow on their peaks, under a blue sky with thin cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Green Lake, 2:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then it was time to head back south. At 3:10, on the way down, the sky had clouded over, and a long range of
    snowy peaks stood above the dark forested hills.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'peaks-southbound.webp'} alt="A range of snow-covered peaks above dark forested hills under a gray sky, seen from the highway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>On the way back south, 3:10 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We were back in Vancouver before five, and went to <strong>Stanley Park</strong>. From Prospect Point at 4:37 the
    Lions Gate Bridge, the green bridge we had driven across the day before, crossed the water below, with
    towers and mountains behind it. At 5:05, by the park's Coal Harbour side, two big horses were hitched to a
    tour carriage decorated with autumn leaves.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lions-gate.webp'} alt="The green Lions Gate suspension bridge over the water, seen through autumn trees, with apartment towers and mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Lions Gate Bridge from Prospect Point, 4:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stanley-park-horses.webp'} alt="Two large gray horses in harness in front of a green tour carriage with a striped canopy, decorated with orange maple leaves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Carriage horses in Stanley Park, 5:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A few steps away, the Coal Harbour marina was perfectly still. The sailboats, the towers of downtown and the
    streaky clouds were all mirrored in the water.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coal-harbour-marina.webp'} alt="A marina of sailboats and motorboats in still water, with the towers of downtown Vancouver behind, all reflected in the water under streaky clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Coal Harbour marina from Stanley Park, 5:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 4: Richmond and home</h2>
  <p>
    Our flight home on Monday was in the evening, and we spent the middle of the day in Richmond. Lunch, at about
    one o'clock, was at a Chinese restaurant: a stir-fry of peppers and meat, squares of tofu with toppings, a soup,
    and a cold drink in a bowl with a small panda face on top.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'panda-drink.webp'} alt="A white bowl with black spots holding a milky drink with green jelly and ice, and a small panda face sitting on top, with a green straw." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A panda in a drink, 12:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we went to a cat café in Richmond. Most of my wife's photos there are of me holding a large orange
    cat, which stay in the family album. One of the others shows a long-haired cat stretching up a scratching post.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'cat-cafe.webp'} alt="A long-haired gray and white cat standing on its hind legs, stretching up a rope-wrapped scratching post with a wooden shelf." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>At the cat café, 3:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Our flight left Vancouver at 5:55 that evening and was due at LAX at 8:52. In Whistler the maples had turned
    red, and there was already snow on the peaks.
  </p>
